/-!
# The top de Rham differential vanishes, in the case of a section

Let `A` be a Noetherian ring and `f : X ⟶ Spec A` proper and smooth of relative dimension
`n = e + 1`, with `A ⟶ Γ(X, 𝒪_X)` bijective and with a section `σ`. Let `W = X ×_A X`, `Δ` the
diagonal, `j = (σ ∘ f, id)` and `j' = (id, σ ∘ f)`, and let `γ_Δ`, `γ_j`, `γ_{j'}` be the Gysin
maps of [Stacks, Tag 0G8A] of these closed immersions on classes of `X`
(`Stacks.WeilDeRham.diagonalGysin`, `Stacks.WeilDeRham.sectionFstGysin`,
`Stacks.WeilDeRham.sectionSndGysin`). This file carries out the argument of
[Stacks, Tag 0G8J] in this case: granted the diagonal claim `γ_Δ(θ) = γ_{j'}(θ) + γ_j(θ)` on
`Hⁿ(X, Ω^{n-1}_{X/A})` (`Stacks.WeilDeRham.DiagonalClaim`) and the injectivity of `γ_j` on
`Hⁿ(X, Ωⁿ_{X/A})` (`Stacks.WeilDeRham.TopInjective`), the de Rham differential
`d : Hⁿ(X, Ω^{n-1}_{X/A}) ⟶ Hⁿ(X, Ωⁿ_{X/A})` vanishes.

The three Gysin maps commute with the de Rham differentials ([Stacks, Tag 0G8B]), so applying
`d` to the diagonal claim gives `γ_Δ(dθ) = γ_{j'}(dθ) + γ_j(dθ)`. On `Hⁿ(X, Ωⁿ_{X/A})` the three
maps agree: they are `A`-linear, they agree on the Gysin class `ξ` of the section, and `ξ`
generates `Hⁿ(X, Ωⁿ_{X/A})` as an `A`-module. Hence `γ_j(dθ) = 2 γ_j(dθ)`, so `γ_j(dθ) = 0` and
`dθ = 0`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.WeilDeRham.gysinCohomologyMap_globalSmul`: the Gysin map on cohomology commutes with
  multiplication by a global function.
* `Stacks.WeilDeRham.ambientCopy_comp_formsGysinMap_comm_deRham`: the Gysin map of a class of
  `X` commutes with the de Rham differentials.
* `Stacks.WeilDeRham.hom_eq_of_span_eq_top`: two `A`-linear maps out of a cohomology group
  generated by one class agree if they agree on it.
* `Stacks.WeilDeRham.diagonalGysin_top_eq_sectionFstGysin`,
  `Stacks.WeilDeRham.diagonalGysin_top_eq_sectionSndGysin`: in top degree the three Gysin maps
  agree.
* `Stacks.WeilDeRham.topDifferentialVanishes_of_diagonalClaim`: the vanishing of the top de Rham
  differential.

## Implementation notes

The Gysin class `ξ` lives in degree `0 + n`, with forms of degree `(0 : ℤ) + n`, the spelling the
Gysin map of [Stacks, Tag 0G8A] produces; the agreement of the three maps is first proved there
and then read in degree `n` by rewriting `0 + n = n` in the statement, which involves no transport
of classes.

The linearity of the Gysin map on cohomology is that of the derived category: multiplication by a
global function `r` on an `𝒪_X`-module `ℳ` is `r • 𝟙`, so for every morphism
`Φ : 𝒜[-c] ⟶ ℱ[0]` of the derived category, which is `Γ(X, 𝒪_X)`-linear, `r[-c] ≫ Φ = r • Φ =
Φ ≫ r[0]`.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0G8J](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0G8J)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0G8B](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0G8B)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

